6 What It Buys in Serving L36:   8. cite26†7 Related Work L37:     1. cite27†Fewer experts at inference. L38:     2. cite28†Sensitive tokens, sinks and super experts. L39:     3. cite29†Where a prompt’s task information sits. L40:   9. cite30†8 Limitations L41:   10. cite31†9 Conclusion L42:   11. cite32†References L43:   12. cite33†A Guide to the Appendices L44:   13. cite34†B Direct Tests of Sink-Related Explanations L45:     1. cite35†Batch. L46:     2. cite36†Statistics. L47:     3. cite37†Conditions. L48:     4. cite38†DeepSeek’s insensitive layout. L49:     5. cite39†Keys and values. L50:     6. cite40†Attention blocks. L51:     7. cite41†Prefix sweep (vLLM). L52:     8. cite42†Prespecified rules and their verdicts. L53:     9. cite43†Where the NLL increase lies (post hoc). L54:     10. cite44†Medians (post hoc). L55:   14. cite45†C Placement Batches: Full Account L56:     1. cite46†Joint batch. L57:     2. cite47†Keeping the interface or the prompt ends full recovers the loss of uniform reduction. L58:     3. cite48†Moving the protected budget into the documents recovers little of the gain. L59:     4. cite49†What the controls identify. L60:     5. cite50†More placements, a second family and the assistant header. L61:     6. cite51†Marks and positions are not distinguished in these templates. L62:     7. cite52†Why positional rules suffice here, and when they would not. L63:   15. cite53†D Serving Measurements L64:     1. cite54†Same batch, same implementation. L65:     2. cite55†The implementation can erase the saving. L66:     3. cite56†No stable speed-up on Qwen3.6. L67:     4. cite57†The cost model and the measured gain. L68:   16. cite58†E Rules, Controls and Implementation L69:     1. cite59†Rules that keep some prompt tokens full. L70:     2. cite60†Prespecification. L71:     3. cite61†Implementation. L72:     4. cite62†Cost. L73:   17. cite63†F Sources of Evidence L74:   18. cite64†G Joint Batch: Further Results L75:     1. cite65†Cycle 1. L76:     2. cite66†Per-task differences. L77:     3. cite67†Marks and positions in the earlier batch. L78:     4. cite68†Form and content of the long-system loss (earlier vLLM 0.10.2 batch, not the joint batch). L79:   19. cite69†H Negative-Control Batch L80:     1. cite70†Design. L81:     2. cite71†Analysis. L82:   20. cite72†I Interface Batch L83:     1. cite73†Design. L84:     2. cite74†Results. L85:   21. cite75†J Placement Batch L86:     1. cite76†Design. L87:     2. cite77†Results. L88:   22. cite78†K Further Analysis L89:     1. cite79†K.1 It is the answer boundary, not a template L90:     2. cite80†K.2 Content: the question, and the paragraphs that hold the answer L91:   23. cite81†L Setup, Statistics and Reproducibility L92:     1. cite82†Engines. L93:     2. cite83†Prompts. L94:     3. cite84†Statistics. L95:     4. cite85†Compute. L96: cite86†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L97: \nL98: arXiv:2610.22454v1 [cs.CL] 15 Oct 2026\nL99: # Where Prefill Expert Reduction Hurts MoE Models:\nL100: A Few Prompt Tokens Carry Most of the Short-Answer F1 Loss\nL101: \nL102: Zhiyuan Wang Affiliation: Independent Researcher Email: zhiyuanwang@sjtu.edu.cn\nL103: ###### Abstract\nL104: Running prompt tokens through fewer routed experts during prefill is a simple way to make mixture-of-experts (MoE) serving cheaper, yet it is unclear what this damages. We locate the damage with token-, layer- and attention-path interventions on short-answer question answering in four MoE families. At half the experts the F1 loss of three families is mostly answer form: the model still finds the answer but stops answering briefly.\nL105: In Qwen3-30B-A3B this loss is made at the two chat-template tokens before the answer, and in DeepSeek-V2-Lite at the first 8–32 prompt tokens, where its instruction sits; by answer likelihood these positions account for about half and a fifth of the damage. Reduction changes the first token’s attention mass, norm and largest activation by less than 4%, and restoring super experts or massive activations recovers at most about a quarter of the loss, well below keeping the sensitive positions full.\nL106: On real prompts with about 4% of tokens at full experts, placing them on the task interface keeps F1 within one point of the unmodified model, while placing them at random in the documents is about 3 points worse (two of five prespecified primary comparisons; the others were undecided or not run).\nL107: Because the answer boundary always ends a single-turn prompt, and DeepSeek’s loss was no longer detectable once its instruction moved away from the start, the sensitive tokens lay at the prompt ends in every sensitive layout we found, so rules that keep the ends are not distinguished from rules that read application marks. Time to first token falls about 14% on 11K-token prompts and not at 2K tokens.\nL108: ## 1 Introduction\nL109: Long retrieval-augmented and agentic prompts make prefill a large part of the time to first token (TTFT). In MoE models [cite87†38 , cite88†9 , cite89†27 ] much of the prefill compute is in the expert layers: 57.8% of the multiply-adds of a 2K-token prefill of Qwen3-30B-A3B and 41.7% at 8K tokens.\nL110: Since prompt tokens mostly build the context that later tokens read, it is tempting to give them fewer experts; ExFold [cite90†36 ] does so for every prompt token and folds the excluded experts’ contribution into the retained ones with calibrated scalar projections, while SlimWise [cite91†28 ] argues that pruning prefill experts trades quality for little throughput and prunes only during decoding. We ask instead what is lost, where, and why.\nL111: Three questions organise the paper. What is lost? (Section cite13†3.1 ) Splitting F1 into precision, recall and answer length shows that at half the experts the F1 loss is mostly form in three of four families. Where is it made? (Sections cite14†3.2 –cite15†3.3 ) Keeping single tokens or spans at full experts shows that in Qwen3, DeepSeek and Qwen1.5 a few tokens at one end of the prompt produce it.\nL112: Why there? (Section cite16†4 ) Attention-path blocking and layer repair in Qwen3 are consistent with these tokens carrying the instruction to the answer, as safety decisions are anchored in template tokens [cite92†22 ] and the refusal direction is read at post-instruction positions [cite93†1 ] in dense chat models.\nL113: Because keeping the prompt ends full resembles StreamingLLM [cite94†37 ], and because the super experts that create attention sinks can be removed by expert compression [cite95†32 ], we also measure directly whether reduction disturbs sinks, massive activations or super experts. Section cite22†5 then tests the consequence on real prompts: at a fixed number of full-expert tokens, does it matter where they go?\nL114: #### Contributions.\nL115: \nL116:   * •\nL117: \nL118: A localisation of the F1 damage of prefill expert reduction on short-answer QA: mainly answer form in three of four families, made at two template tokens before the answer in Qwen3-30B-A3B, within the first 8–32 tokens of DeepSeek-V2-Lite’s standard layout and at the last two tokens in Qwen1.5-MoE; in Qwen3 it follows whatever tokens precede the answer, not one template (Section cite12†3 ). In answer NLL the same positions account for only part of the damage.\nL119: \nL120:   * •\nL121: A mechanism account for Qwen3, consistent with, but not uniquely identified by, path-blocking and repair experiments, and prespecified measurements in two families that find the first-token sink statistics unchanged and super experts and massive activations unable to account for most of the loss (Section cite16†4 ).\nL122: \nL123:   * •\nL124: Equal-budget tests on real prompts with prespecified primary comparisons: a small full-expert budget helps on the task interface and little when scattered at random in the documents; in our templates rules that keep the ends are not distinguished from marks, so the result is a placement principle rather than a new selector (Section cite22†5 ).\nL125: ## 2 Setting\nL126: #### Prefill expert reduction.\nL127: In each MoE layer a router scores $E$ experts per token and the token runs its $K$ highest-scoring ones ($E{=}128$, $K{=}8$ in Qwen3-30B-A3B). We write $K\\to k^{\\prime}$ for a reduced token that keeps its $k^{\\prime}$ highest-weight experts with unchanged weights. Generated tokens always run $K$, and shared experts are never reduced. The last prompt position computes the logits of the first answer token, so reducing it also reduces the first decoding step.\nL128: Uniform reduction applies $K\\to k^{\\prime}$ to every prompt token; the unmodified model runs $K$ everywhere; a rule may keep some prompt tokens full (all $K$ experts). Unless stated, reduction halves the experts ($8\\to 4$ for Qwen3, $6\\to 3$ for DeepSeek); single-token experiments use $8\\to 2$, where single tokens have measurable effects, and say so.\nL129: #### Models, tasks and pipelines.\nL130: Qwen3-30B-A3B (reasoning disabled) is the main model; DeepSeek-V2-Lite-Chat (64 routed experts, top-6, two shared experts), Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B-Chat and OLMoE-1B-7B-Instruct are the other families. The analysis task is HotpotQA [cite96†39 ] in the distractor setting: an instruction to give only the short answer, two demonstrations, ten paragraphs and the question (about 1,800 prompt tokens), scored by F1 with greedy decoding.\nL131: Token, layer and attention-edge interventions run in a HuggingFace pipeline that reproduces the engine’s results; equal-budget comparisons run in vLLM [cite97†19 ] (Appendix cite81†L ). Differences are paired per question with their reference and carry 95% bootstrap intervals over questions. Loss sizes differ between batches and sample sizes; each number names its batch where it matters, and Appendix Table cite98†7 lists the source of every claim.\nL132: #### Prespecification.\nL133: For four batches—three quality-only placement batches (Section cite22†5 ) and the direct-measurement batch (Section cite18†4.2 )—the prompt manifest, the conditions, the primary and secondary comparisons and their decision rules were written into the batch’s analysis script before the formal run; we call these comparisons prespecified. The joint quality-and-latency batch and the analysis experiments of Sections cite12†3 and cite17†4.1 are exploratory.\nL134: There is no external registry, later batches were designed knowing earlier results, and comparisons added after seeing the data are marked post hoc. The decision rules of primary comparisons used a two-point margin or, for necessity, a threshold of half of the interface rule’s gain over uniform reduction (2 points, half the joint batch’s gain, in the negative-control batch; 1.30, half the same batch’s gain, in the placement batch); secondary comparisons were tests against zero with Holm correction.\nL135: The one-point non-inferiority margin used in Section cite22†5 is the resolution of the joint batch’s full-budget control and was adopted after seeing the data, as in the earlier vLLM 0.10.2 batches; it is stricter than the prespecified two points.\nL136: ## 3 What Is Lost, and Where\nL137: \nL138: The interventions in this section use unmarked templates and their own baselines; Section cite22†5 turns to real marked prompts. Section cite13†3.1 uses 2,000 HotpotQA questions, on which the unmodified Qwen3 scores F1 69.81; the span experiments of Section cite14†3.2 and the family batch of Table cite99†1 are separate 2,000-question runs, in which the same uniform $8\\to 4$ costs $-$3.38 and $-$3.34, and single-token experiments use 500 questions.\nL139: ### 3.1 At half the experts, most of the F1 loss is form\nL140: F1 conflates two failures, so we split it into the precision and recall of the gold answer’s words and track the answer length (Figure cite100†1 ). With uniform $8\\to 4$, recall changes by $-$1.09 points and precision by $-$4.87: the answer is found nearly as often as before, but the model writes more around it (2.49 words against 2.33 on average). The larger part of what is lost is compliance with the instruction to answer briefly; about a point of containment is content (Appendix cite80†K.2 ).\nL141: At $8\\to 2$ both fall, and the losses separate by place: reducing only the last eight prompt tokens costs 10.31 points of precision and 0.53 of recall, with answers twice as long, while reducing everything but those eight tokens leaves the length intact and costs both recall ($-$4.78) and precision ($-$6.09).\nL142: GSM8K [cite101†8 ] shows the form loss too: uniform reduction to two experts loses 14.09 points in the earlier unmarked batch, while only 84.1% of outputs still end with the required “####” marker; with the last two tokens kept full, 97.7% do. F1 is length-sensitive and the instruction asks for a short answer, so “form” here means compliance with that request; answer-NLL measurements in Section cite18†4.2 show that the damage is less concentrated by that measure.\nL143: Figure 1: Form and content separate. Qwen3-30B-A3B, HotpotQA, 2,000 questions; change against the unmodified model. (a, b) Reducing every prompt token mostly lowers precision; reducing only the last eight tokens lowers only precision; reducing all but the last eight lowers recall and precision alike. (c) The answer lengthens exactly when the last tokens are reduced.\nL144: ### 3.2 In Qwen3 the form is decided at the two tokens before the answer\nL145: The damage is not spread over the prompt (Figure cite102†2 ). At $8\\to 4$, keeping the last two tokens full and reducing the rest moves the F1 loss from $-$3.38 to $-$1.06 [$-$2.10, $-$0.01], and a tail of 32 changes F1 by +0.63 [$-$0.21, +1.47] (in the family batch the last two give $-$1.01 [$-$2.07, +0.02], an interval touching zero); eight full tokens at the start ($-$2.78) or at random positions ($-$3.27) recover little.\nL146: Reducing tokens in isolation at $8\\to 2$ (500 questions), the last eight cost 8.02 F1 points and the last two alone cost the same ($-$8.07), while the six before them cost 0.70; the two back each other up, the last costing 0.14 and the second-to-last 1.40 when reduced singly.\nL147: Reducing the last position alone, which computes the first decoding step, therefore costs little; because the two back each other up, this does not show how much of the joint effect runs through the last position’s own computation of the first answer token (Section cite18†4.2 ). With reasoning disabled a Qwen3 prompt ends in <|im_start|>assistant\\n<think>\\n\\n</think>\\n\\n: the two tokens are the closing tag of the empty reasoning block and the blank line after it, which carry no task content as inputs.\nL148: In Qwen3 it follows the answer boundary, not one template (Appendix cite79†K.1 ). With a base model and no template, where the prompt ends in “Answer:”, reducing the last two tokens at $8\\to 2$ costs 5.69 F1 points, again mostly precision.\nL149: With reasoning enabled the tokens before the answer are generated, run all experts, and reducing the prompt end has no detectable effect ($-$0.12 [$-$1.23, +1.01]); replaying the same reasoning trace as a prompt makes its last two tokens reducible, and reducing them costs 10.62 points.\nL150: Figure 2: Which tokens need their experts (Qwen3-30B-A3B, HotpotQA F1 against the unmodified model). (a) Keeping the last $N$ prompt tokens full: the two-token boundary accounts for much of the format effect; a 32-token tail preserves the question as well. (b) The same number of full tokens placed last, first, or at random on this template. (c) Reducing only the named tokens to two experts: the damage of the last eight is the damage of the last two, which are redundant with each other.\nL151: ### 3.3 Other families: one end or the other\nL152: Table cite99†1 summarises the same interventions at half of each model’s experts (details in Appendix Table cite103†14 and Figure cite104†5 ). In DeepSeek-V2-Lite the form loss is made at the other end: reducing only the first 20 tokens reproduces it ($-$9.45, family batch) while the sequence-start token alone is harmless (+0.11 [$-$0.54, +0.76]); what is left is the instruction, “Answer the question using the documents.\nL153: Give only the short answer.” Its tail acts in the opposite direction to Qwen3’s: reducing only the last two tokens shortens answers and raises F1 by 3.66. A vLLM prefix sweep on 400 questions (uniform loss $6.79$ in that batch) keeping the first 1, 8 or 32 tokens full recovers $-0.42$, $+3.80$ [$+1.88$, $+5.88$] and $+8.33$ [$+5.63$, $+11.21$] points against uniform reduction; a later run on the same manifest adds $-0.07$ for 2 tokens and $+1.39$ [$-0.36$, $+3.13$] for 4 (Appendix cite34†B ).\nL154: With ten to 300 words of unrelated text before the instruction, or with it between the documents, uniform reduction shows no detectable loss ($-1.9$ to $+1.8$; the ten-word interval [$-4.88$, $+2.01$] is wide; vLLM, 400 questions of the placement batch), so in DeepSeek’s standard layout we cannot tell whether the sensitivity follows the instruction or the prompt start.\nL155: Qwen1.5-MoE loses content rather than form—its answers get shorter and F1 rises, but the gold answer is found less often—and reducing only its last two tokens reproduces its containment loss, while keeping them full leaves about a quarter of it (Appendix Table cite103†14 ). OLMoE’s loss is spread over both ends. With 32 tokens kept full at each end, none of the four models shows a detectable HotpotQA loss in that batch (Qwen1.5’s F1 rises by 1.9 and its containment is unchanged; not an equivalence test).\nL156: Table 1: Where the loss is made, by family (HotpotQA, half the experts, earlier unmarked batch; change against the unmodified model with 95% intervals; DeepSeek’s 8–32 comes from the vLLM 0.31 prefix sweep of Section cite15†3.3 ). Qwen1.5’s loss is measured by containment (whether the gold answer appears), since its F1 rises.\nL157: Model  | Uniform reduction  | What is lost  | Where it is made  | First and last 32 full\nL158: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL159: Qwen3-30B-A3B  | F1 $-$3.34 [$-$4.45, $-$2.26]  | form  | last two tokens  | $-$0.41 [$-$1.23, +0.39]\nL160: DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | F1 $-$8.87 [$-$10.38, $-$7.37]  | form  | first 8–32 tokens (instruction or prompt start)  | +0.95 [$-$0.17, +2.12]\nL161: Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B  | containment $-$4.50 (F1 +2.13)  | content  | last two tokens  | containment +0.05 [$-$1.25, +1.35]\nL162: OLMoE-1B-7B  | F1 $-$5.11 [$-$6.76, $-$3.43]  | form (precision)  | both ends, beyond the last two  | $-$0.94 [$-$2.25, +0.34]\nL163: ## 4 Why These Tokens\nL164: ### 4.1 In Qwen3 the boundary is consistent with carrying the instruction to the answer\nL165: Three measurements on the unmodified model fit one account (Figure cite105†3 ). Attention. The query that predicts the first answer token attends to the second-to-last prompt token 15 times more per token than to a question token; attention is not importance, though, since the last token is read three times more than the second-to-last and matters far less when reduced (Section cite14†3.2 ). Path blocking.\nL166: Blocking single attention paths without reducing any expert, the first answer token depends on the last two tokens seeing the instruction (+6.21 nats) and the documents (+3.35), and barely on their seeing the question (+0.43); the stop token depends on the answer seeing the last two prompt tokens (+20.70 nats), not the documents (+0.64) or the question (+0.74). Hiding as many random prompt tokens from the answer costs nothing (Appendix cite34†B ).\nL167: A block removes a heavily attended token outright, a far larger perturbation than reduction (which raises the answer NLL by 1.42 nats in the 200-question batch of Section cite18†4.2 ), so blocking shows what flows through the boundary tokens, not how reduction damages it. Layer repair.\nL168: With the last eight tokens at two experts, restoring all experts in one band of four layers repairs the most, about half, for layers 20–23 and 24–27 (each leaves 46% of the damage), and at most a tenth in the first twelve or last eight layers; three partial repairs make the damage larger (above 100% in Figure cite105†3 d), so mixed reduced and repaired states do not add up monotonically.\nL169: These results are consistent with middle-layer experts writing “how to answer” into the boundary tokens and the answer reading it there, as safety decisions are anchored in template tokens of dense chat models [cite92†22 ]; they do not identify the mechanism uniquely.\nL170: Figure 3: Why the last tokens matter in Qwen3. (a) The account. (b) Attention each token receives from the query that predicts the first answer token (log scale). (c) Increase in the loss of the model’s own answer when one attention path is blocked: the first answer token needs the last two prompt tokens to see the instruction and documents; the stop token needs the answer to see those two tokens.\nL171: (d) Damage left after repairing one band of layers when the last eight tokens run two experts: the mean increase in the answer’s NLL as a share of the increase with no repair, over 300 prompts. A value above 100% means that the partial repair made the loss larger; the 95% prompt-bootstrap interval of this ratio excludes 100% for experts kept in layers 0–3 (120% [103, 141]) and 44–47 (113% [105, 121]) and for the clean state restored in layers 8–11 (126% [107, 148]).\nL172: Repairing one band mixes repaired and reduced computation, a state the model sees under neither condition. The ratio is of means, which a few prompts dominate.\nL173: ### 4.2 Reduction does not disturb the sink statistics we measured\nL174: Sinks coincide with massive activations on the first token and on delimiters [cite106†33 , cite107†13 , cite108†3 , cite109†29 ], and in Qwen3-30B-A3B these are produced by a few routed super experts, whose removal collapses the sinks [cite95†32 ].\nL175: $K\\to k^{\\prime}$ keeps a token’s highest-weight experts, so super experts are unlikely to be removed, but Qwen3’s two sensitive tokens are heavily attended delimiters and could be secondary sinks, so we measured the question directly in a prespecified HuggingFace batch: DeepSeek-V2-Lite and Qwen3-30B-A3B on 200 standard-layout HotpotQA questions each (Appendix cite34†B ; uniform reduction costs $10.3$ F1 points in DeepSeek and an undetectable $-1.06$ [$-4.41$, $+2.33$] in Qwen3 there).\nL176: Super experts are identified as in cite95†Su et al. [32] . Each intervention is scored by the share of the uniform-reduction increase in answer NLL (of the unmodified model’s own answer) that it removes, which is far less noisy than F1 at this size; Qwen3’s rows rest on NLL alone.\nL177: Table 2: Direct tests of sink-related explanations (HuggingFace, 200 HotpotQA questions per model; DeepSeek $6\\to 3$, Qwen3 $8\\to 4$). Change: ratio of the statistic under uniform reduction to the unmodified model, minus one, averaged over layers 1 onward and heads (first token: attention from the last eight prompt positions, hidden norm, largest absolute channel; last eight prompt tokens: attention from the answer, mean norm and largest channel; for DeepSeek these are not its sensitive tokens).\nL178: Recovery: share of the uniform-reduction increase in answer NLL removed, $\\overline{\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{unif}}-\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{cond}}}/\\overline{\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{unif}}-\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\text{orig}}}$, in percent; rules keep 32 (DeepSeek) or 8 (Qwen3) tokens full. Minus position: paired difference to keeping the sensitive positions full (post hoc, except for the two signal rules, for which it is the quantity of the prespecified rule (h)).\nL179: Restorations and random full tokens mix clean and reduced states (Section cite18†4.2 ). 95% bootstrap intervals over questions. Answer NLL rises from 1.10 to 2.64 nats (DeepSeek) and 0.22 to 1.64 (Qwen3).\nL180:  | DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | Qwen3-30B-A3B\nL181: --- | --- | ---\nL182: Is the sink disturbed? (change under uniform reduction)\nL183: ---\nL184: First token: attention / norm / largest channel  | $+0.3$% / $-0.2$% / $-0.1$%  | $+1.5$% / $-3.2$% / $-3.2$%\nL185: Last eight tokens: attention / norm / largest channel  | $-4.3$% / $-4.3$% / $-1.4$%  | $-0.3$% / $-8.3$% / $-10.0$%\nL186: Hide first token from last eight and answer, $\\Delta$NLL (random token)  | $+1.07$ ($+0.00$) nats  | $+40.6$ ($+0.01$) nats\nL187: Super experts (layer/expert); boundary selections removed  | L3/E54, L4/E38; 12.5%  | L1/E68, L2/E92, L3/E82; 10.8%\nL188:  | Recovery  | Minus position  | Recovery  | Minus position\nL189: Keep the sensitive positions full (first 32 / last 8)  | $+22$ [$+10$, $+33$]  | —  | $+49$ [$+30$, $+65$]  | —\nL190: Restore super experts on every reduced token  | $+1$ [$-4$, $+6$]  | $-20$ [$-31$, $-9$]  | $+8$ [$-11$, $+24$]  | $-41$ [$-64$, $-20$]\nL191: Restore massive activations to clean values  | $-4$ [$-9$, $+1$]  | $-25$ [$-36$, $-14$]  | $+6$ [$-10$, $+22$]  | $-43$ [$-64$, $-22$]\nL192: Keep tokens full by super-expert signal  | $+2$ [$-4$, $+7$]  | $-19$ [$-30$, $-9$]  | $+13$ [$-6$, $+30$]  | $-35$ [$-60$, $-12$]\nL193: Keep tokens full by massive-activation signal  | $+5$ [$-3$, $+12$]  | $-17$ [$-30$, $-3$]  | $+4$ [$-14$, $+20$]  | $-44$ [$-69$, $-23$]\nL194: Keep random tokens full  | $+6$ [$0$, $+11$]  | $-16$ [$-27$, $-5$]  | $-14$ [$-33$, $+2$]  | $-63$ [$-86$, $-40$]\nL195: Restore the first token’s keys and values  | $0$ [$-6$, $+6$]  | —  | $-17$ [$-38$, $0$]  | —\nL196: Restore the sensitive positions’ keys and values  | $+19$ [$+8$, $+30$]  | —  | $-353$ [$-527$, $-247$]  | —\nL197: #### The first-token sink statistics are unchanged.\nL198: Both models have a functioning first-token sink—hiding it from the last eight prompt tokens and the answer raises the answer NLL by 1.1 nats in DeepSeek and 40.6 in Qwen3, against nothing for a random token—yet uniform reduction changes its attention mass, norm and largest activation by at most 3.2%; the prespecified rule (a), defined on DeepSeek, reads excluded, and Qwen3’s first-token statistics are within the same band.\n"
